  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/38883c7cc4bfe8c9ecccadfcc2d0c75ff66f79b19ff697c15ddbe53f4c63dbab.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with a lid using $216\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?",
  "worked_solution": "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $216\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}=216\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =216\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{216\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{r} \\\\ V(r) & =(108r-{r}^{3})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(108-3{r}^{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (108-3{r}^{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 108 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=6$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=-6\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(6)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=6\\,\\text{cm}.$",
